  <title>The kinds of brick</title>
  <p><gui>New Brick</gui>, in the <gui>Bricks</gui> tab, lists the kinds of
  brick in three groups. A new brick is named after its kind and the first
  free number, as <sys>sw1</sys> or <sys>tap1</sys>, and its settings show at
  once.</p>
  <p>When this computer lacks the program of a kind, its row says so after a
  warning sign. You can still make the brick: it starts once the program is
  installed. See <link xref="programs"/>.</p>
  <section id="machines">
    <title>Machines and switches</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Virtual machine</gui></title>
        <p>A computer that QEMU runs, with its disks, its memory, and a
        network card for each switch it plugs into. See
        <link xref="machines"/>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Switch</gui></title>
        <p>A VDE switch: the other bricks plug into its ports, as into an
        Ethernet switch. It can start as it is. It runs
        <cmd>vde_switch</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Switch wrapper</gui></title>
        <p>A switch that another program runs. The bricks of this project
        plug into its socket, whose path its settings give. You don't start
        or stop it: it runs while that socket is there.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Router</gui></title>
        <p>A VDE router between switches. It has no settings yet: its console
        configures it. It runs <cmd>vde_router</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="links">
    <title>Links</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Wire</gui></title>
        <p>A cable between two switches, which its settings choose. It runs
        <cmd>dpipe</cmd> and <cmd>vde_plug</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Netemu</gui></title>
        <p>A cable between two switches that emulates a network link: its
        bandwidth, delay, loss and more, in states that change over time. It
        runs <cmd>vde-netemu</cmd>, or <cmd>wirefilter</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Tunnel server</gui></title>
        <p>One end of an encrypted tunnel: it joins a switch here to a tunnel
        client on another computer, which connects to it. It runs
        <cmd>vde_cryptcab</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Tunnel client</gui></title>
        <p>The other end of an encrypted tunnel: it joins a switch here to a
        tunnel server on another computer, whose address its settings give.
        It runs <cmd>vde_cryptcab</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
  </section>
  <section id="host">
    <title>This computer</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Tap</gui></title>
        <p>A network interface of this computer, plugged into a switch: this
        computer joins the lab through it. It runs <cmd>vde_plug2tap</cmd> as
        root, through <cmd>sudo</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Capture interface</gui></title>
        <p>An interface of this computer, whose packets go to a switch. It
        runs <cmd>vde_pcapplug</cmd> as root, through <cmd>sudo</cmd>.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>For what <cmd>sudo</cmd> needs, see <link xref="problems#sudo"/>.</p>
  </section>
</page>
